<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Cranmer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Cranmer was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 1533 to 1555 and a leader of the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. He annulled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>'s marriage to Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, and under Edward VI he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> and gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> a Protestant doctrine. Under the Catholic Mary I he was tried for heresy and burnt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in 1556.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer.jpg" alt="Thomas Cranmer" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerlach Flicke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Cranmer_by_Gerlach_Flicke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church of England, created by the Reformation and given its liturgy in <b>Thomas Cranmer</b>’s Book of Common Prayer, became the mother church of the worldwide Anglican Communion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>New texts gave the churches their shape: Luther’s German Bible and his Small Catechism of 1529, the Augsburg Confession drawn up by Philip Melanchthon in 1530, <b>Thomas Cranmer</b>’s Book of Common Prayer of 1549 and Reformed statements of faith such as the Heidelberg Catechism of 1563.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>…hymn has been part of Anglican liturgies since the ordinal of 1550, in a version attributed to Archbishop <b>Thomas Cranmer</b>, his only venture into English verse and the only metrical hymn of the Edwardian liturgy; Cranmer added a stanza on the Last Judgement and strife within Christendom that…</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aslockton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.95,-0.9&amp;year=1532" aria-label="Aslockton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4530 340 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas Cranmer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4980 610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4980 610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.95,-0.9&amp;year=1532">What was here in 1532? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And forasmuch as my hand hath offended, writing contrary to my heart, therefore my hand shall first be punished; for when I come to the fire, it shall first be burned.&#8221;</p><footer>Last speech at St Mary's, Oxford, before his burning, 1556 &#183; original &#183; as John Foxe recorded it in his Book of Martyrs &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22400/pg22400-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whither, then, may I go, or whither shall I flee? To heaven I may be ashamed to lift up mine eyes, and in earth I find no place of refuge or succor.&#8221;</p><footer>His last prayer before his burning, Oxford, 21 March 1556 &#183; original &#183; as Foxe recorded it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/On_the_Eve_of_His_Execution" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To do good unto all men, as much as in you lieth, and to hurt no man, no more than you would hurt your own natural loving brother or sister.&#8221;</p><footer>His last exhortation before his burning, Oxford, 21 March 1556 &#183; original &#183; as Foxe recorded it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/On_the_Eve_of_His_Execution" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That you set not your minds overmuch upon this deceitful world, but upon God, and upon the world to come…&#8221;</p><footer>His last exhortation before his burning, Oxford, 21 March 1556 &#183; original &#183; as Foxe recorded it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/On_the_Eve_of_His_Execution" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That next unto God you obey your king and queen willingly and gladly, without murmuring or grudging…&#8221;</p><footer>His last exhortation before his burning, Oxford, 21 March 1556 &#183; original &#183; as Foxe recorded it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/On_the_Eve_of_His_Execution" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
